WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.640
If there's one thing that the Harry&nbsp;
Potter movies did exceptionally well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.280
it was casting the right actors for the&nbsp;
series' many substantial roles. Despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:14.080
the intimidating number of memorable characters&nbsp;
featured throughout the original Potter books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:18.800
the films always consistently managed to&nbsp;
cast talented actors who were ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.440
well-suited to their roles. That wasn't just&nbsp;
the case with the series' older figures either,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:28.080
many of whom were played by legendary British&nbsp;
stage and screen actors, but also with its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.880
younger characters. Emma Watson, Rupert Grint,&nbsp;
and Daniel Radcliffe, for instance, were all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.000
cast at young ages to play the franchise's&nbsp;
central trio, and the three actors grew into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.960
talented screen performers over the course of&nbsp;
the series' eight film installments. Each of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:48.000
the three have gone on to have successful acting&nbsp;
careers outside of the Potter franchise as well.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.280
However, the Harry Potter franchise ended up&nbsp;
having to recast a handful of roles across its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:58.800
eight installments. In fact, fans may be surprised&nbsp;
to learn that, in the case of Lavender Brown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.520
the memorable character ended up being&nbsp;
played by a total of three different actors.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:09.280
Though she's not credited, actor Kathleen Cauley&nbsp;
originally played Lavender Brown in 2002's Harry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:14.480
Potter and the Chamber of Secrets. Two years&nbsp;
later, in 2004's Harry Potter and the Prisoner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:19.280
of Azkaban, the character was played by Jennifer&nbsp;
Smith, who had no lines but appeared in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.480
background a handful of times throughout the film.&nbsp;
When Lavender became a major character in Harry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.280
Potter and the Half-Blood Prince, though, she&nbsp;
was played by Jessie Cave. She went on to reprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:35.840
the role two more times in Harry Potter and the&nbsp;
Deathly Hallows Part 1 and in its 2011 follow-up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.800
Deathly Hallows Part 2. Many have often wondered&nbsp;
why the role was played by three different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.160
actors in the Potter films, but the folks at&nbsp;
Fandom think the explanation is pretty clear.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:51.040
As they tell it, the Harry Potter and the&nbsp;
Half-Blood Prince novel, which makes Lavender a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:57.120
major character for the first time, was released&nbsp;
in July 2005 after both the Chamber of Secrets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.840
and Prisoner of Azkaban films were made. That&nbsp;
means that, at the time of their release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:07.040
the teams behind those two films didn't know that&nbsp;
Lavender would be a major character in the future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:12.080
Therefore, they likely just assigned the character&nbsp;
name to Cauley and Smith in the Chamber of Secrets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.280
and Azkaban films to help better organize&nbsp;
those films' various background players.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.480
ScreenRant theorizes that since Lavender's&nbsp;
profile rose in the sixth installment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.920
the producers went with a more&nbsp;
established actor in Cave.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.800
Yet these explanations haven't stopped fans&nbsp;
from raising concerns about the recasting.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.280
Harry Potter film fans know that the Lavender&nbsp;
Brown character grew in her impact on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:40.160
overall storyline in the sixth movie when&nbsp;
she became a love interest for Ron Weasley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.240
following his triumphant first match&nbsp;
as the Gryffindor quidditch keeper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:49.920
As chants of "Weasley" echo in the Gryffindor&nbsp;
common room, Jessie Cave's Lavender Brown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:55.840
rushes in and starts making out with Ron. What&nbsp;
those who've not read the books may not realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:03.840
is that the snogging was described as being&nbsp;
"impossible to tell whose hands were whose."

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:03.898
“Lovely.”

00:03:03.898 --> 00:03:08.240
That might sound like a bit much for a young&nbsp;
adult franchise, but some have taken it literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:13.440
as an indication that Lavender and Ron have the&nbsp;
same skin color, though it's entirely possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:18.320
the passage was meant figuratively to simply&nbsp;
describe how intense the cuddle session was.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:23.520
Regardless of how one interprets that particular&nbsp;
line, it's indisputable that Lavender was first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:29.360
played by two Black actors when the character had&nbsp;
no lines and was recast with a white actor when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:34.560
the character took on a larger role in Half-Blood&nbsp;
Prince. Off Colour said it’s worth pointing out,

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:38.960
"The tone-deafness of recasting a role&nbsp;
that had been played by two Black actresses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:42.160
with a white actress as soon as&nbsp;
the character is given a line.”

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:43.040
They concluded,

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.200
"[The recasting was] especially bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.600
since Harry Potter doesn't accurately&nbsp;
represent Britain's great diversity."

